/*
PROBLEM:
LeetCode 3876 - Construct Uniform Parity Array II

PROBLEM UNDERSTANDING:

We are given an array nums1 containing n distinct integers.

We have to construct another array nums2 of the same length
such that all elements of nums2 have the same parity.

This means either:
1. All elements must be EVEN.
2. All elements must be ODD.

For every index i, we have exactly two choices:

1. Keep the original element:
   nums2[i] = nums1[i]

2. Subtract another element:
   nums2[i] = nums1[i] - nums1[j]

   Here, j must be different from i.

IMPORTANT CONDITION:
The subtraction result must be greater than or equal to 1.

------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT PARITY RULES:

1. Even - Even = Even
2. Odd - Odd = Even
3. Even - Odd = Odd
4. Odd - Even = Odd

Therefore:

x - ODD  = Parity changes.
x - EVEN = Parity remains the same.

However, we must also ensure that the subtraction result
is positive.

------------------------------------------------------------

APPROACH:
Mathematical Observation + Minimum Element

ALGORITHM:

STEP 1:
Check whether the array contains at least one ODD element.

If no odd element exists, all elements are EVEN.

We can simply keep all elements unchanged.
Therefore, return true.

STEP 2:
Check whether the array contains at least one EVEN element.

If no even element exists, all elements are ODD.

We can simply keep all elements unchanged.
Therefore, return true.

STEP 3:
Now we know that the array contains BOTH odd and even elements.

Find the minimum element of the array.

There are two possibilities:

CASE A: Minimum element is ODD.

We can make all elements ODD.

1. Keep every odd element unchanged.
2. For every even element, subtract the minimum odd element.

Why does this work?

The minimum element is odd, and every even element
must be greater than it because all elements are distinct.

Therefore, the subtraction is always positive.

Also:

EVEN - ODD = ODD.

Hence, all resulting elements become ODD.

Return true.

CASE B: Minimum element is EVEN.

We cannot construct a valid array.

Why?

Consider the smallest ODD element in the array.

1. We cannot keep it unchanged because we want all elements
   to have the same parity as the even elements.
2. We cannot subtract a smaller ODD element because no such
   element exists. Otherwise, that smaller odd element would
   be the minimum of the entire array, which is even.
3. Subtracting an EVEN number does not change its parity.
4. Subtracting a larger number is not allowed because the
   result would be less than 1.

Therefore, the smallest odd element cannot be converted
into an even number.

Return false.

------------------------------------------------------------

TIME COMPLEXITY:

O(n)

1. First loop checks for an odd element: O(n).
2. Second loop checks for an even element: O(n).
3. min_element() finds the minimum element: O(n).

Total = O(n) + O(n) + O(n) = O(n).

AUXILIARY SPACE:

O(1)

We are using only a few variables and no extra data structure.
*/

class Solution {
public:
    bool uniformArray(vector<int>& arr) {

        // Get the total number of elements in the array
        int n = arr.size();

        // ------------------------------------------------
        // STEP 1: Check whether at least one ODD element exists
        // ------------------------------------------------

        int i = 0;

        for (; i < n; i++) {

            // Get the current element
            int ele = arr[i];

            // If ele % 2 == 1, the element is ODD
            // We have found at least one odd element
            if (ele % 2 == 1) {
                break;
            }
        }

        // If i reaches n, it means no odd element was found.
        // Therefore, all elements are EVEN.
        // We can keep every element unchanged.
        if (i == n) {
            return true;
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------
        // STEP 2: Check whether at least one EVEN element exists
        // ------------------------------------------------

        int j = 0;

        for (; j < n; j++) {

            // Get the current element
            int ele = arr[j];

            // If ele % 2 == 0, the element is EVEN
            // We have found at least one even element
            if (ele % 2 == 0) {
                break;
            }
        }

        // If j reaches n, it means no even element was found.
        // Therefore, all elements are ODD.
        // We can keep every element unchanged.
        if (j == n) {
            return true;
        }

        // ------------------------------------------------
        // STEP 3: Both ODD and EVEN elements exist.
        // Now find the smallest element in the array.
        // ------------------------------------------------

        // min_element() returns an iterator pointing to
        // the smallest element.
        // * is used to get the actual value.
        int mini = *min_element(arr.begin(), arr.end());

        // ------------------------------------------------
        // STEP 4: Check the parity of the minimum element
        // ------------------------------------------------

        // If the minimum element is EVEN,
        // it is impossible to construct a uniform parity array.

        // Reason:
        // Consider the smallest ODD element.
        // There cannot be any smaller ODD element to subtract
        // from it, because the minimum element of the entire
        // array is EVEN.

        // Subtracting an EVEN number does not change parity.
        // Subtracting a larger number would give a result < 1.

        if (mini % 2 == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // If the minimum element is ODD:
        // Keep all ODD elements unchanged.
        // Subtract this minimum ODD element from every EVEN element.
        //
        // Every EVEN element is greater than mini because
        // all array elements are distinct.
        //
        // EVEN - ODD = ODD.
        // Also, every subtraction result is positive.
        //
        // Therefore, all elements can be made ODD.

        return true;
    }
};
